Here are my findings.

## 1. Business-day / registration-date implementation (main)

**File:** `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/ResolveRegistrationDate.java`

This is the single class that adjusts the registration date onto a business day. It is an OfficeFloor step function. Full contents:

```java
public class ResolveRegistrationDate {

    public void service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto request, Out<LocalDate> registrationDate)
            throws FutureRegistrationDateException {
        LocalDate effective = request.getRegistrationDate();
        if (effective == null) {
            effective = LocalDate.now();
        }
        else if (effective.isAfter(LocalDate.now())) {
            // A supplied date later than the server date is rejected; back-dating is allowed.
            throw new FutureRegistrationDateException(effective);
        }
        // A weekend rolls forward to the next Monday.
        while (effective.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SATURDAY
                || effective.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SUNDAY) {
            effective = effective.plusDays(1);
        }
        registrationDate.set(effective);
    }
}
```

Key behavior (also documented in its Javadoc):
- Effective date = supplied `registrationDate`, else `LocalDate.now()`.
- A future date (after server date) is rejected via `FutureRegistrationDateException` (→ HTTP 400); back-dating is allowed.
- Weekend (Saturday/Sunday) rolls forward to the next Monday. **NOTE: the production code only skips weekends — it does NOT skip public holidays.**

**Supporting classes (same package / escalation):**
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/FutureRegistrationDateException.java` — thrown for future-dated requests (message: "Registration date must not be later than the server date").
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler.java` — global handler returning 400.
- Downstream consumers of the adjusted date: `CheckDailyLimit.java` (daily create-limit per adjusted business day) and `BuildOwner.java` / `AssignMembershipNumber.java` (membership year segment uses adjusted year).

There is **no** public-holiday handling anywhere in `src/main` — the only holiday logic lives in the test tree (see below).

## 2. Tests related to registration date / business day

**File:** `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` (full contents):

```java
package org.springframework.samples.petclinic.acceptance;

import java.time.DayOfWeek;
import java.time.LocalDate;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertTrue;

/** holiday-business-day: the default registration date rolls past public holidays as well as
 * weekends. The specific holiday calendar and the server clock can't be driven black-box, so assert
 * the invariant that subsumes it: the default registrationDate is never a Saturday or Sunday. */
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {

	@Test
	void coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay() throws Exception {
		DayOfWeek dow = LocalDate.parse(
				fetchOwner(createOwnerOk(structuredOwner())).get("registrationDate").asText()).getDayOfWeek();
		assertTrue(dow != DayOfWeek.SATURDAY && dow != DayOfWeek.SUNDAY, dow.toString());
	}
}
```

This is a black-box acceptance test extending `AcceptanceBase`. It only asserts the weekend invariant (not holidays), because the calendar/clock can't be driven black-box. There is **no dedicated unit test** for `ResolveRegistrationDate` (no `ResolveRegistrationDateTest` exists).

## 3. Public-holiday handling

Public-holiday logic exists **only in the test base class**, not in production code.

**File:** `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` (relevant snippet, lines ~239–274):

```java
/** Fixed public holidays the business-day roll skips (year of the run). */
protected static final Set<LocalDate> HOLIDAYS = Set.of(
		LocalDate.parse("2026-01-01"), LocalDate.parse("2026-01-26"),
		LocalDate.parse("2026-04-25"), LocalDate.parse("2026-12-25"), LocalDate.parse("2026-12-28"));

/** Roll a date forward over weekends and pinned holidays to the next business day. */
protected LocalDate toBusinessDay(LocalDate d) {
	while (d.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SATURDAY || d.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SUNDAY
			|| HOLIDAYS.contains(d)) {
		d = d.plusDays(1);
	}
	return d;
}
```

The test base pins an Australian-style holiday set (New Year, Australia Day, ANZAC Day, Christmas, Dec 28) and a `toBusinessDay` helper that skips both weekends and holidays. Note the discrepancy: the test comment/helper expect holiday-skipping, but the production `ResolveRegistrationDate` only skips weekends — so holidays are an intended-but-unimplemented behavior on the main side.

The third test file, `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AuditLogCapture.java`, is unrelated to date logic.

## Build tool and how tests are run

- **Build tool:** Maven, with the wrapper `./mvnw` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/mvnw`, `mvnw.cmd`) and `/home/daniel/sandbox/pom.xml`. Parent is `spring-boot-starter-parent`; it uses the OfficeFloor Spring Boot starter (`net.officefloor.springboot:officefloor-rest-spring-boot-4-starter`, version `4.0.2`).
- **Test stack:** `spring-boot-starter-test` (JUnit 5 / Jupiter), `mockito-core`, and OfficeFloor's `woof_test` for black-box HTTP acceptance testing.
- **Running:** the pom does not declare explicit surefire/failsafe configuration, so standard Maven lifecycle applies:
  - `./mvnw test` — run unit/acceptance tests
  - `./mvnw clean install` (readme uses `mvn clean install`) — full build + tests
  - `./mvnw spring-boot:run` — run the app
  - Run the single relevant test: `./mvnw test -Dtest=AcceptanceTest`

The `readme.md` references `./mvnw spring-boot:run` and `mvn clean install`; `AGENTS.md` documents the OfficeFloor step-function model (the `@Val`/`Out`/`next:` step wiring that `ResolveRegistrationDate` participates in) but does not give a distinct test command.

